<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<configuration>

  <!--
    Sources are cleared and restated, rather than inherited from whatever the machine has
    configured. A developer's user-level NuGet.config commonly carries private feeds from other
    work, and without this they sit in front of nuget.org for every restore here. That is not a
    hypothetical: this repository was being built on a machine with an authenticated
    organisation feed enabled globally.

    Two things follow from leaving that in place, and both are worse than the inconvenience of
    pinning. A restore that succeeds locally can fail in CI, which has no such feed - so the
    build that matters disagrees with the build that is quick to run. And a package name that
    exists on both sources resolves from whichever answers first, which is the substitution
    hazard the ecosystem calls dependency confusion. Neither is likely here; both cost nothing
    to remove.
  -->
  <packageSources>
    <clear />
    <add key="nuget.org" value="https://api.nuget.org/v3/index.json" protocolVersion="3" />
  </packageSources>

  <!--
    Cleared for the same reason, and separately, because clearing sources does not clear these.
    A user-level source mapping survives the block above and then applies to sources it no
    longer has, and NuGet refuses the whole command rather than ignoring it. The first sign of
    this is the tool install in the README failing with a complaint about source mapping, which
    is a confusing way to learn that the machine has an organisation feed configured for
    unrelated work.

    Note for whoever edits this next: no CLI flag may be written here. A double hyphen inside
    an XML comment makes the file invalid, and NuGet reports that as "NuGet.Config is not valid
    XML" with no indication of which line.
  -->
  <packageSourceMapping>
    <clear />
  </packageSourceMapping>

</configuration>
